WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.720
Numbers from input should not be converted with routines that hide failure.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:09.700
A function such as atoi cannot reliably tell the difference between a real zero and a failed conversion.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:23.200
A safer pattern uses strtol, strtoul, or related functions at a high level: reset errno when appropriate, parse the token, inspect the end pointer, and confirm that the value ended where the format says it should end.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.500
Conversion is only the first question.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.240
The second question is whether the converted number is valid for the program.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.500
Range validation must be tied to the purpose of the value.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:42.720
A port, count, array index, timeout, allocation size, offset, percentage, and enum value all have different valid ranges.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.700
The C type system will not automatically enforce those domain rules.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.600
Signed and unsigned conversions are especially easy to get wrong.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.540
A negative value converted to an unsigned type can become a very large value.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.520
A value that fits in a long may not fit in an int, a size_t, or a protocol field.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.020
Validate before narrowing or mixing signedness.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.760
Integer overflow becomes dangerous when a value is used to size memory or advance through a buffer.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:18.100
A calculation such as count times element size can wrap around and allocate less memory than later code expects to fill.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.320
Offset plus length can wrap and appear to be inside a buffer when it is not.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.340
Check calculations before allocation, indexing, copying, or pointer movement.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:40.840
Treat counts, lengths, offsets, and indexes as untrusted until every relationship has been checked: nonnegative where required, within the allowed maximum, and consistent with the actual data available.
